    "q-340463": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340463",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x-2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x+2x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.",
