    "q-340386": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340386",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-6x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+6x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{18}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-1-0)-\\frac{1}{18}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
